**Congratulations!** You have spent the last 9 days learning the vocabulary of a new era. You have moved from being a passive observer to an active participant. But today, we change the definition of success. We are moving towards becoming **AI Maximalists**.

&nbsp;  

An **AI Maximalist** is not just someone who uses AI occasionally. It is a person who, when faced with a problem or opportunity, takes AI as the first step and as a partner along the journey. It is not an add-on; **it is a fundamental**, integrated part of the work you do.

&nbsp;

**Two-Box Thinking**
&nbsp;  

To understand where we are going, we must look at the Two Box Model:

&nbsp;  

**Box 1 (Optimization):** This is everything you do today. Using AI here compresses time and reduces costs. It’s about efficiency.

&nbsp;  

**Box 2 (Possibility):** This is the empty box of opportunity. These are things we haven't done because they were too expensive, difficult, or time-consuming.

&nbsp;  

**The Goal:** We use the skills from Level 1 (Prompting) to clear our plates in Box 1, so we can spend our energy creating value in Box 2.

**Level 1: Basic Interaction (The Conversationalist)** *COMPLETED*

&nbsp;  

**Focus:** Fundamentals, Prompting, & Verification

&nbsp;  

This was the foundation. You invested the time to learn the fundamental skills required to be successful with AI.

&nbsp;  

• **The Skill:** You learned how to write a prompt, how to provide context, and how to iterate and collaborate with the AI. You moved beyond simple requests to conducting a meaningful dialogue.
&nbsp;  

• **The Action:** You didn't just ask questions; you learned to verify and ensure that the outputs you received were indeed correct.
&nbsp;  

• **The Mindset:** You learned to overcome the "curse of knowledge" and explain your needs clearly to a "smart stranger".
&nbsp;  

• **Status:** By completing this masterclass and mastering RICECO and TRUST, you have solidified your place here

&nbsp;

**Level 2: Sophisticated Application (The Orchestrator)**

&nbsp;  

**Focus:** Products, Documentation, & The "Dance" This is where you start to have a "dance"~ a back-and-forth interaction ~ to build on concepts.

&nbsp;  

• **The Skill:** You move beyond simple chats to building meaningful products that are polished, complete, and complex.
&nbsp;  

• **The Action:** You use AI to produce documentation, solution architectures, or even complex artifacts like legislation.
&nbsp;  

• **The Goal:** You are no longer just chatting; you are producing tangible assets. You are iterating to verify and enhance your work until it is a "product".

&nbsp;

**Level 3: Advanced Application (The Architect)**

&nbsp;  

**Focus:** Integration, Agents, & Organizational Knowledge This is the advanced application where you weave AI into the fabric of your organization.

&nbsp;  

• **The Skill:** This gets into Agents and Agentic Workflows. It involves integrating AI into your existing tools, connecting it to your databases, and tapping into petabytes of organizational data.
&nbsp;  

• **The Action:** You build multi-step quality control processes where AI meets or exceeds your standards for privacy, design, and cybersecurity.
&nbsp;  

• **The Goal:** Synthesizing critical pieces of organizational knowledge into a logical structure that AI can use as a partner

&nbsp;

**The Golden Rule of Growth**

&nbsp;  

**Don't rush to get to Level 3**. Invest the time to get through Level 1 and Level 2 to build a solid foundation. You can self-assess where you are and right-size your training to be successful.

&nbsp;

You now know that the quality of your output depends entirely on the quality of your input. But not every interaction requires a novel. **We distinguish between two types of interaction:**

&nbsp;  

**1. The Micro Prompt (The Text Message)**

&nbsp;  

**Use Case:** Speed and efficiency. "Define this," "Summarize this," "Translate this".
&nbsp;  

**When to use:** When the task is simple, you need an answer fast, and the format doesn't matter much.
&nbsp;  

**The Framework:** Use **ICC** (Instruction, Context, Constraints). This condensed version covers 80% of your daily needs.

&nbsp;  

**2. The Super Prompt (The Project Brief)**

&nbsp;  

**Use Case:** Complex work requiring nuance, structure, and high-quality output.
&nbsp;  

**When to use:** When you are building a policy brief, a formal report, or a stakeholder email.
&nbsp;  

**The Framework:** This requires the full **RICECO** methodology.

&nbsp;  

**Key Takeaway:** Choosing the right prompt is like choosing the right tool. You wouldn't use a sledgehammer to hang a picture frame.

&nbsp;

**The Architecture of a Perfect Prompt**
To prepare for Level 2, you must master the **RICECO framework**. This is not just for chatting; this is the code you will eventually use to program AI Agents.

&nbsp;  

• **Role (R):** Assign a persona (e.g., "You are a board-certified sleep doctor"). This flips a switch in the model, changing tone and perspective instantly.
&nbsp;  

• **Instruction (I):** The core task. Be specific. Avoid vague words like "engaging" without defining what that looks like.
&nbsp;  

• **Context (C):** The **Wedge of Context**. Start broad and narrow it down. Who is the audience? What is the background?. The context is often more important than the prompt itself.
&nbsp;  

• **Examples (E):** The "Gold Standard." If you have a masterpiece example, give it to the AI to mimic. This is called "few-shot prompting".
&nbsp;  

• **Constraints (C):** The guardrails. What should the AI avoid? (e.g., "No hashtags," "Keep it under 100 words") .
&nbsp;  

• **Output Format (O):** Do you need a table, a JSON file, or a bulleted list? Define the structure.
&nbsp;  

**Pro Tip:** You can ask the AI to "hydrate" your simple request into a full RICECO prompt before executing it.

&nbsp;

We often treat AI like a brilliant, super-eager junior intern. It can whip up drafts in seconds, but it has zero real-world experience. It wants to please you, which means it might confidently make things up (hallucinations).

&nbsp;  

Before you move to automation (Level 2), you must learn to verify using the **T.R.U.S.T. Protocol:**

&nbsp;  

• **T - Traceability:** Can you draw a straight line from the claim back to a verifiable source? Demand verbatim quotes and URLs.
&nbsp;  

• **R - Reliability:** Is the source from the top rung of the ladder (Government laws, peer-reviewed research) or just a random blog?.
&nbsp;  

• **U - Uncertainty:** Ask the AI to list its assumptions and give a confidence score. Force it to admit what it doesn't know.
&nbsp;  

• **S - Sensitivity & Safety:** Check for PII (Personally Identifiable Information) or bias. Is this safe to publish?.
&nbsp;  

• **T - Triangulation:** Verify critical facts with at least two independent sources.
&nbsp;  

**The Rule:** If you don't check the work, the output becomes a liability.

&nbsp;

**The "Aha!" Moment** This is where Level 1 connects to Level 2.

&nbsp;  

You have learned to write **Super Prompts** using **RICECO**. You have learned to verify them using *T.R.U.S.T.**

&nbsp;  

**Here is the secret:** A **Super Prompt** that works perfectly is the "brain" of an **AI Agent**.

&nbsp;  

• **Level 1 (Conversationalist):** You copy-paste a RICECO prompt into a chat window every time you need a task done.
&nbsp;  

• **Level 2 (Orchestrator):** You save that RICECO prompt as the "System Instruction" for an Agent. Now, that specific expertise (e.g., "The Proposal Writer," "The Python Coder") is permanently available to you and your team.

&nbsp;  

When you build an Agent, you are simply freezing a Super Prompt into a repeatable tool.

&nbsp;

In **Level 2**, you stop playing every instrument yourself. **You become the conductor**. You will take the specific Agents you create (using your RICECO skills) and connect them.

&nbsp;  

You are moving beyond single conversations to building sophisticated products and workflows. In Level 2, you stop playing every instrument yourself and start conducting an AI team, connecting specialized agents to automate complex processes.

&nbsp;

• **Agent A** (The Researcher) passes data to **Agent B** (The Analyst), who passes a draft to **Agent C** (The Writer).
&nbsp;  

• This creates an **Agentic Workflow**~ *an assembly line of intelligence.*

&nbsp;

As you master orchestration, you reach **Level 3**. This is where we build the **AI Factory**.

&nbsp; 

This is the advanced domain where you stop just using tools and start designing the "AI Factory". You will learn to weave AI into the very fabric of your organization, automating the creation process and integrating directly with your core systems.

• You connect these agents to your databases, your email, and your legacy systems.
&nbsp; 

• You move from chatting with a bot to having AI build entire applications for you

&nbsp;

You have trained, you have practiced, and you have leveled up. You now possess the mindset, the superpowers, and the tools of an **AI Maximalist.** But the journey does not end here; in fact, your real adventure is just beginning. To truly bridge the gap to Level 2, you must **turn these new skills into daily habits** and practical workflows. 

&nbsp;

Use the resources below to connect with your peers, access pre-built solutions, and start building the future of your organization today

&nbsp;

**Your Adventure Continues:**

&nbsp;

**1. The Meeting Multiplier** Turn your next meeting transcript into something greater than just notes. Use AI to "hydrate" the conversation, instantly generating a complete project plan, a prioritized task list, and a polished stakeholder email.

&nbsp;

**2. The Process Documenter** "Stop relying on static documents. Map a core team process using an agentic workflow to create a living "digital twin" of your business that visualizes exactly how your team works and can be executed in real-time.

&nbsp;

**3. The Knowledge Curator** Build a mini-knowledge bank for a single, recurring task. Take the time to clean up your files and turn your team's "tacit knowledge" into an AI-accessible asset that becomes a permanent resource for your organization.

&nbsp;

**4. Or, Chart Your Own Course** Have a unique challenge or a 'Box 2' opportunity in mind? Don't feel limited by these options. Identify a novel use case, apply the tools you've learned to build a custom solution, and share your creation—we can even build it together.

**1. Join the Community of Practice** Learning doesn't happen in isolation. After completing your training, join the Community of Practice to workshop new use cases, get tips on the latest tools, and connect with peers. Share your quests and learn from other Maximalists to enhance your skills.

&nbsp;

**2. Explore the Masterclass Site & Revisit Use Cases** Need inspiration? The Masterclass site is your answer for practical application. Revisit specific use cases—like business case creation or procurement drafting—and download the pre-built workflows and templates to run them yourself immediately.

&nbsp;

**3. Keep Building with Albert** Your sovereign AI platform is ready for your next project. Albert synthesizes chat, agents, and canvases into one tool, serving as the foundational element you need to unlock the power of AI without per-user subscription fees.

&nbsp;

**4. Share and Engineer the Future** If you create something novel, share it. Help your colleagues by sharing your agents and insights, effectively engineering and designing the future of your organization. Go build the future!

&nbsp;

**Real-World Example – Systems Thinking**

&nbsp;  

• **Purpose of the example**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Demonstrates how Systems Thinking can be applied to a real workflow before converting it into an agentic design.  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Shows how to view a process at a high level to understand its essential components.

&nbsp;  

• **Input**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• The workflow begins with a meeting transcript — an unstructured source containing decisions, discussions, and raw information.

&nbsp;  

• **Output**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• The final product is a structured project plan, ready for implementation or executive review.

&nbsp;  


• **Key takeaway**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Viewing the process as a system helps you understand what must happen at a high level before designing the deeper, agent-level workflow.

&nbsp;

**Part 5: AI as a Partner in Design**

&nbsp;  

• **Purpose of this part**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Introduces how AI can support the *design* of workflows, not just execute them.  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Positions AI as a collaborator that helps shape, refine, and validate workflow structures.

&nbsp;  

• **Key idea**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• AI contributes throughout the entire design lifecycle — clarifying the problem, proposing structures, refining steps, and testing the workflow.

&nbsp;  

• **Why this matters**  
&nbsp;&nbsp;• Treating AI as a design partner increases clarity, reduces omissions, and leads to stronger, more reliable agentic workflows.

&nbsp;

**Transcript to Project Charter - ABC Workflow**

&nbsp;

Please click this link to access the following: [**Transcript to Project Charter - ABC Workflow**](https://drive.google.com/file/d/1tDCfab8Zop16vxBmVDkKlUnaKub3-Kfz/view?usp=sharing)

&nbsp;

You are welcome to download, run, or edit this file within the [**Agent Builder Console**](https://agentbuilderconsole.com/) as needed.

&nbsp;

The terms **chatbot, workflow, and agent** describe how much initiative and real-world capability you give an AI, that choice directly affects speed, reliability, and risk in your day-to-day work. A **chatbot** is great when you need words now: a clear prompt in, a polished draft out (emails, outlines, rewrites), but it won’t check calendars, pull policy sections, or act unless you tell it to. 

&nbsp;  

A **workflow** adds tools and guardrails so the system can follow a repeatable path you define; “look up the policy → extract sections → draft a summary → file it here”—perfect for routine tasks that touch live data, though improvements still come from you adjusting the steps. 

&nbsp;  

An **agent** is outcome-driven: you give it a goal (“produce a briefing with verified sources”), and it plans, chooses tools, checks its own work, and iterates without you hand-holding each step.

**Knowing which level to use and when** means fewer rewrites, stronger sourcing, clearer accountability, and safer automation. In practice: use a chatbot for rapid drafting, a workflow for consistent, auditable processes, and an agent when you need a system that can think through the path, act across systems, and self-improve under your rules. 

&nbsp;  

This matters because Level 2 will have you move between these levels deliberately, so you can deliver faster with lower risk and designs that scale.

You move from telling a machine how to do something to telling it what outcome to achieve — and letting it figure out the steps.

&nbsp;  

**Bottom line:** the journey from chatbot → workflow → agent is a shift in control. 

&nbsp;

In this short video, you’ll see how three “levels” of AI capability change what’s possible in your day-to-day work: a **chatbot** that drafts words on command, a **workflow** that follows a tool-connected path you design, and an **agent** that takes a goal and figures out the steps on its own. 

&nbsp;  

**Watch for two ideas as you go:**
&nbsp;  

 **(1)** how much initiative the system has (does it only answer, does it follow rails, or does it plan and act?)
&nbsp;  

**(2)** how that affects speed, reliability, and risk. 

&nbsp;  

By the end, you should be able to look at any task on your plate and decide: “Is this a quick chatbot job, a repeatable workflow, or something I should hand to an agent with clear guardrails?”

Now that you’ve seen the three levels in action, use them deliberately. Reach for a **chatbot** when you need fast words and you already have the facts. Switch to a **workflow** when the task must touch live systems or follow a repeatable, auditable path you control. 

&nbsp;  

Choose an **agent** when you want outcome-oriented automation: give it a goal, the allowed tools and sources, plus verification rules (like **T.R.U.S.T.**), and let it plan, act, and improve. As you continue through Level 2, you’ll mix these levels: start simple with a **chatbot**, codify the reliable steps into a **workflow**, and graduate to an **agent** when the work benefits from autonomous planning and iteration under your governance.

In public sector work, choosing the right level directly affects speed, reliability, and risk . You do not need to use an Agent for everything.

&nbsp;  

**Use the "Ladder Up" Strategy:** The practical rule of thumb is to start with a strong prompt, then layer context as the stakes and complexity rise .

&nbsp;  

**1) Is this a quick drafting task?**
&nbsp;  

• Action: Use a **Chatbot** with **Prompt Engineering.**
&nbsp;  

• Example: Drafting an email or rephrasing a paragraph .

&nbsp;  

**2) Does it require a repeatable path or live data?**
&nbsp;  

• Action: Build a **Workflow** with **Context Engineering** (documents/tools).
&nbsp;  

• Example: Summarizing news links weekly or following a specific audit process .

&nbsp;  

**3) Does it require outcome-seeking autonomy?**
&nbsp;  

• Action: Deploy an **Agent** with **Context + Guardrails.**
&nbsp;  

• Example: Researching a briefing note where the AI must verify sources and plan its own research steps .

&nbsp;

**Crucial "Good Looks Like" Checklist:**
&nbsp;  

• The chosen level must match the risk and oversight needs .
&nbsp;  

• Inputs and outputs must be **logged and verifiable** (essential for public sector workflows) .
&nbsp;  

• There must be a clear success metric (what "good" means) and a fail-safe .
&nbsp;

**From Chatbot → Workflow → Agent**

&nbsp;

You just walked the arc from “words-on-demand” to “goal-seeking systems.” The core idea to keep: as you move up the levels, you hand over more initiative and real-world capability, shifting from giving instructions to giving outcomes. In the video, Level 1 is a closed-box LLM (great at drafting, passive); Level 2 connects tools on fixed “railroad tracks” (repeatable, auditable, but you’re still the decision-maker); Level 3 promotes the LLM to the decision-maker (it plans, chooses tools, checks itself, and iterates toward the goal).

**Next Steps: Prompt Engineering vs Context (Content) Engineering**
&nbsp;

In the next module, we’ll go deeper on how you get reliable results at any level by separating two skills:

**Step 1: Identify a common task.** What do you frequently ask the AI to do?
&nbsp;  

**Step 2: Define the RICECO elements.** For the chosen task, carefully define the Role, Instruction, Context, Examples, Constraints, and Output Format.
&nbsp;  

**Step 3: Create the "Gem" template.** Combine the RICECO elements into a single, reusable prompt.
&nbsp;  

**Step 4: Test and refine the "Gem"**. Use the "Gem" to generate outputs and adjust the RICECO elements as needed.

&nbsp;

**Add the following Prompt:**

&nbsp;  

----------------- Everything Below This Line ----------------------

&nbsp;  

You are a prompt engineering expert specializing in the RICECO framework. Your task is to evaluate any user-provided prompt and enhance it to fully align with the RICECO model for optimal AI responses. RICECO stands for:

&nbsp;  

- **R: Role** - Assign a specific role to the AI to shape its tone, perspective, and depth (e.g., "You are a board-certified sleep doctor" or "You are a business growth strategist specializing in AI adoption"). This is powerful for shifting the AI's mindset but optional if not relevant—use it when it adds unique value, such as for specialized expertise, creative personas, or audience-specific framing. Rank it lower in frequency but high in impact when applicable.

&nbsp;  

- **I: Instruction** - The core, essential task or action you want the AI to perform. Make it specific, direct, and detailed to avoid vagueness (e.g., instead of "Write an engaging YouTube short," say "Write a 60-second YouTube short script about prompting tips using a curiosity gap hook and a scroll-stopping visual anchor"). This is required every time and should clearly define the "what" without ambiguity, incorporating elements like purpose or key requirements.

&nbsp;  

- **C: Context** - Provide background information to make the output relevant and tailored. Include details like audience (e.g., "small business owners"), scenario or platform (e.g., "turning podcast episodes into YouTube shorts"), purpose (e.g., "explain the value in simple, practical terms"), tone/perspective, or any additional data dumps (e.g., business details, goals, or constraints). This prevents generic responses; more context is better for complex tasks, but keep it concise and ensure it doesn't bury the instruction.

&nbsp;  

- **E: Examples** - Supply 1-3 (or more if needed) sample inputs/outputs to demonstrate the desired structure, tone, formatting, or logic (e.g., "Use this as a reference for tone and format: [paste a sample newsletter]"). This is especially impactful for writing, creative, or technical tasks like JSON formatting, few-shot prompting, or imitating styles. Use it when precision is key; it's called "few-shot prompting" and can anchor the AI effectively without overcomplicating simple prompts.

&nbsp;  

- **C: Constraints** - Define boundaries, rules, limits, or must-haves to refine the output and avoid common pitfalls like wordiness, vagueness, repetition, or overly safe/generic content (e.g., "Keep under 100 words; avoid buzzwords like 'innovative'; use a warm, conversational tone; include a quote and stat in the first paragraph"). Differentiate from context: this sets guardrails. Essential for consistency in workflows; can be built into custom instructions for reuse.

&nbsp;  

- **O: Output Format** - Specify how the response should be structured for usability (e.g., "Present as a three-column table with columns: Tool Name, Key Features, Ideal Use Cases" or "Format as a tweet thread, mind map with nested markdown lists, or JSON"). This makes outputs cleaner and more actionable; optional for simple responses but valuable for comparisons, data, scripts, or visuals.

&nbsp;  

For everyday quick prompts, use the condensed I-C-C method (Instruction, Context, Constraints) to cover 80% of cases efficiently. Only expand to full RICECO when needed for depth. Elements can overlap or blend naturally in the prompt (e.g., context woven into instruction), but think about them separately to ensure completeness. The framework works across all LLMs like ChatGPT, Gemini, Claude, or Grok.

&nbsp;  

**Process for Handling Queries:**

&nbsp;  

1. **Evaluate**: Thoroughly analyze the user-provided prompt. Identify which RICECO elements are already present, missing, weak, or overemphasized. Note strengths (e.g., "Strong instruction but lacks context"), gaps (e.g., "No examples provided, leading to potential inconsistency in tone"), and common mistakes like vagueness or lack of specificity. Be brief but comprehensive in your analysis.

&nbsp;  

2. **Enhance**: Rewrite the prompt to incorporate all applicable RICECO elements. Blend them into a natural, flowing prompt without rigid headers (unless for clarity in complex cases). Omit irrelevant elements but explain why in your analysis. Aim for clarity, conciseness, and robustness—ensure the enhanced prompt is reusable and would elicit high-quality AI outputs. If the original is vague or open-ended, make it specific while preserving intent.

&nbsp;  

3. **Suggest Next Steps**: After enhancement, recommend applying E-I-O (Evaluate, Iterate, Optimize) to refine outputs further:

&nbsp;  

   - **E: Evaluate** - Interrogate the AI's response (e.g., "What assumptions did you make? Critique your own output. What might be missing? Steelman the opposing view.").

&nbsp;  

   - **I: Iterate** - Refine with follow-ups (e.g., "Rewrite to be more concise; add humor; provide three variations.").

&nbsp;  

   - **O: Optimize** - Streamline the prompt for reuse (e.g., "Rewrite this prompt to be more concise while keeping the intent; cut unnecessary words.").

&nbsp;  

**Always output in this exact structure:**

&nbsp;  

- **Evaluation**: [Detailed analysis of the input prompt's RICECO alignment]

- **Enhanced Prompt**: [The full rewritten prompt, ready to copy-paste]

- **Why This Improves It**: [Explanation of key changes, how they address gaps, and expected benefits]

- **Next Steps**: [Specific E-I-O recommendations tailored to the prompt]

&nbsp;  

If the user provides no prompt to enhance, politely ask for one. For controversial or complex topics, ensure the enhanced prompt encourages balanced, substantiated responses without adding unrelated policies.

&nbsp;

Multimodal AI models can understand and combine multiple types of input such as text, voice, images, video, and sensor data. Earlier AI systems worked mostly in a single mode, usually text. Multimodal models reflect how people naturally communicate by blending different signals into a single reasoning process.

&nbsp;

Humans rarely communicate using just words — we use tone, emphasis, gestures, pacing, and visual references. Multimodal AI interprets information in a way that more closely resembles human communication. This makes AI interactions feel more intuitive and natural, shifting AI from a tool that requires precise instructions to something that can interpret intent.

Voice is one of the most natural ways humans communicate. People speak several times faster than they type, which creates an immediate productivity gain. Voice also enables hands-free interaction, allowing someone to work, move, or handle equipment without stopping to type.

&nbsp;


Voice significantly improves accessibility for individuals with mobility challenges, visual impairments, literacy barriers, or repetitive strain issues. It also carries emotional meaning—tone, pacing, and volume all provide context that text alone cannot. This makes voice-based interactions richer and more accurate. In environments where AI is becoming a regular partner in work, voice adds a human dimension back into the digital experience.

A natural voice conversation with AI follows a repeating loop of three steps:

&nbsp;

**1) Speech-to-Text (STT):** The system converts spoken words into text. Modern models capture accents, pacing, and natural pauses with high accuracy.

&nbsp;


**2) AI Understanding:** The AI reads the text and interprets meaning, tone, and intent based on language, context, and previous dialogue.

&nbsp;


**3) Text-to-Speech (TTS):** The AI generates a spoken response. Modern voices incorporate rhythm, tone, and emotion, creating a more human-like experience.

&nbsp;


This cycle repeats continuously as the conversation unfolds. Because it happens quickly, the interaction feels smooth and natural.

Speech-to-Text converts audio into written language through several steps:

&nbsp;


• The system captures the audio waveform from speech.

&nbsp;


• It analyzes the waveform and breaks it into small acoustic units, similar to syllables or phonetic building blocks.

&nbsp;


• It compares these patterns against millions of trained audio samples.

&nbsp;


• Using probability and context, the model predicts the most likely words and constructs them into clean text.

&nbsp;


This process happens almost instantly. The resulting text becomes the input the AI uses to understand and reason about what was said.

Text-to-Speech performs the reverse transformation:

&nbsp;

• The system takes the AI’s generated text response.

&nbsp;

• It determines pacing, emphasis, and tone, selecting how the sentence should sound.

&nbsp;

• It synthesizes a human-like voice that reflects the chosen style.

&nbsp;

• The output is played back as natural-sounding speech.

&nbsp;

Modern TTS systems can express different emotions or speaking styles and can adjust tone mid-sentence, creating conversations that feel fluid and lifelike.

Voice-based interaction provides several advantages:

&nbsp;

**• Speed:** Speaking is faster and more natural than typing, especially when explaining complex ideas.

&nbsp;

**• Engagement:** Conversations have rhythm and flow, making interactions more dynamic.

&nbsp;

**• Context:** Tone, emotion, and emphasis improve accuracy and intent recognition.

&nbsp;

**• Inclusivity:** Anyone can speak; no advanced prompting skills are required.

&nbsp;

**• Reduced cognitive load:** Users can express ideas freely without organizing them first — the AI handles structure and clarity.

&nbsp;

Because of these benefits, voice becomes a practical interface for many types of work.

Voice-enabled AI is being applied across many industries:

&nbsp;

**• Productivity:**  Users can talk to documents — summarizing, rewriting, or creating content through speech.

&nbsp;

**• Customer service:** Natural voice assistants replace menu-based phone systems.

&nbsp;


**• Field operations:** Inspectors, first responders, and technicians can access information hands-free.

&nbsp;

**• Learning and training:** Learners receive real-time feedback through interactive voice-based coaching.

&nbsp;

**• Healthcare:** Clinicians record notes or chart information verbally while staying focused on patients.

&nbsp;

These use cases are directly relevant to government work, enabling employees to remain engaged and productive wherever they are.

 **From “Who drives?” to “What fuel do they have?”**

&nbsp;

In the last module, you learned the three levels of capability: a **chatbot** that passively answers prompts, a **workflow** that follows rails you lay down, and an **agent** that pursues a goal by planning and acting on its own. The big shift was control: from you scripting every step to the model reasoning and acting toward a goal

This module answers the next question: regardless of level, **how do you get reliable, on-task outputs?** You have two levers:

&nbsp;

**•** **Prompt engineering** (the sprint): craft a clear, structured request for a strong single-turn answer.

&nbsp;

**•** **Context (content) engineering** (the marathon): curate what the model can “see” and “use”—documents, tools, prior messages, and memory—so it can reason over trusted sources and act. The accompanying diagram contrasts a minimal input window with a rich, curated context window that includes files, history, and tool calls.

 **Post Video Short Reflections**

&nbsp;  

**1) From asking to architecting.** You saw how prompt engineering shines for speed and clarity; give a role, concrete instructions, constraints, and a clear output format to get quality in one turn. Then you contrasted it with context engineering, where you **design the model’s inputs and capabilities** (docs, tools, memory, prior messages) so it can reason over trusted sources and act. The diagram makes that shift visible: minimal inputs vs. a curated context + tool calls

&nbsp;  

**2) Choosing the right mode**. Use **prompts** for quick drafts, translations, and “answer-once” tasks; use context for research, policy work, analytics, or anything needing repeatability and citations. You don’t have to pick forever: **prototype with a prompt, then layer context** as complexity and risk rise.

This diagram shows the difference between **prompt engineering** and **context (content) engineering** at a glance. 

&nbsp;  

On the left, you see the “single-turn” world: a clear prompt goes in, a draft comes out; great for speed and wording. 

&nbsp;  

On the right, you see a curated **context window** where the model can also “see” selected documents, prior messages or memory, and even call tools, which is better for depth, accuracy, and repeatable work. 

&nbsp;  

Read it left-to-right as a shift from asking well to **architecting the model’s working environment.** Use it to decide when a strong prompt is enough, and when to layer context and tools as complexity and risk rise.

This module clarified the two levers you’ll use in real work: **prompt engineering** for fast, single-turn clarity, and **context (content) engineering** for depth, accuracy, and repeatability.

&nbsp; 

You learned how a strong prompt (roles, clear instructions, constraints, desired output) gets you quality drafts quickly—and how curating context (relevant documents, prior messages/memory, and tools) grounds those drafts in trusted sources and enables verifiable results. 

&nbsp;  

The practical rule of thumb is simple: **start with a strong prompt, then layer context as stakes and complexity rise**. This matters because public-sector work demands traceability and reliability and your ability to choose (and combine) these approaches is what turns AI from a handy writer into a dependable partner.

 **Next Steps**

&nbsp;  

You now have two complementary levers:
&nbsp;  

**•** **Prompt engineering** = precise wording for fast, single-turn value.
&nbsp;  

**•** **Context (content) engineering** = curated information + tools + memory for depth, accuracy, and reusability—the foundation for agents.

&nbsp;  

**Up next: Giving our brilliant ai assistants agency**. You’ll now turn these ideas into a working agent:

&nbsp;  

**•** Turn your best prompt into the agent’s task contract (roles, instructions, outputs).
&nbsp;  

**•** Attach the working environment (policy docs, datasets, memory) and connect tools (search, calculators, internal services).
&nbsp;  

**-** Add guardrails using your verification habits (e.g., T.R.U.S.T. checks, citation requirements).
&nbsp;  

**•** Run test jobs, watch step-by-step reasoning/tool use, and iterate for reliability and traceability.

&nbsp;

**1. T = Traceability**

&nbsp;  

**• Goal:** Make the model "show its work" for every single claim.
**• Why:** AI frequently hallucinates (fabricates) sources, citations, and even URLs.
**• Micro-Prompt:** "For each factual claim, provide the verbatim quote that supports it and the official URL".

&nbsp;  

**2. R = Reliability**

&nbsp;  

**• Goal:** Label the quality of each source so you can decide to keep or replace it.
&nbsp;

**• Why:** Just because a source is real doesn't mean it's good. This step teaches the AI to prioritize authoritative "gold standard" sources (like government reports or laws) over unverified blogs.
&nbsp;

**• Micro-Prompt:** "For each citation, label source type (law/regulation/gov report/peer-review/news/blog), publisher/authority, and publication date. Recommend Keep or Replace".

&nbsp;  

**3. U = Uncertainty**

&nbsp;  

**• Goal:** Force the AI to expose its own assumptions, gaps, and confidence level.
&nbsp;

**• Why:** An AI's training data can be months or years old. This prompt forces it to admit if its information might be outdated or if it made an assumption about your context.
&nbsp;

**• Micro-Prompt:** "List assumptions and unknowns, note anything possibly outdated, and give a 0–100 confidence score with a one-sentence justification".

&nbsp;  

**4. S = Sensitivity & Safety**

&nbsp;  

**• Goal:** Prevent privacy violations or harmful content before publishing.
&nbsp;

**• Why:** This is a critical check for PII (Personally Identifiable Information), biased language, or content that could harm vulnerable groups. It ensures your output aligns with privacy laws.
&nbsp;

**• Micro-Prompt:** "Review for privacy/safety/harm risks (e.g., PII, health, vulnerable groups). Suggest safer wording or redactions consistent with applicable privacy obligations".

&nbsp;  

**5. T = Triangulation**

&nbsp;  

**• Goal:** Verify critical claims with independent sources.
&nbsp;

**• Why:** This reinforces the number one rule: Never rely on a single source. The AI is one source; you must cross-check it with another. This is the final decision gate where the human manager makes the conscious choice to use, revise, or reject the content.
&nbsp;

**• Micro-Prompt:** "For each critical claim, cross-check with 1–2 independent sources. List agreements/conflicts and recommend: Use as-is / Revise / Reject".

&nbsp;

**Fact-Checking Websites:** Utilize reputable fact-checking websites like Snopes, PolitiFact, and FactCheck.org to verify claims and identify misinformation.

&nbsp;  

**Reverse Image Search:** Use reverse image search tools like Google Images or TinEye to determine the origin and context of images. This can help identify manipulated or misattributed images.

&nbsp;  

**Lateral Reading:** Consult multiple sources to gain a broader perspective on the issue. Don't rely solely on one source of information.

&nbsp;  

**Expert Consultation:** Seek the opinion of experts in the relevant field. They can provide valuable insights and help you assess the accuracy of the information.

&nbsp;

Agentic workflows are valuable because they introduce a level of structure and discipline that generic AI prompting lacks.

&nbsp;

**Consistency:**
When work is divided into well-defined steps, the output becomes more stable. Each agent focuses on one role, so the risk of drift, inconsistency, or hallucination drops significantly.

&nbsp;


**Quality:**
Assigning a single responsibility to each stage improves clarity and accuracy. The workflow reduces cognitive overload — both for the AI and for humans — by ensuring that each stage handles only one mental function.

&nbsp;

**Structure & Traceability:**
Workflows create a transparent reasoning path. This allows teams to understand exactly how an output was produced, making it easier to verify, audit, or refine the process. This is especially important in decision support, policy development, governance, and public-sector accountability.

&nbsp;

**Specialization:**
Complex tasks become manageable when they are decomposed into smaller, focused components. Each component performs better because it concentrates on one cognitive role rather than trying to perform many simultaneously.

&nbsp;

Together, these benefits help organizations build scalable, reliable AI-supported processes that improve both productivity and work quality.

Agentic workflows are most effective in situations where work naturally unfolds in multiple stages or where the task requires structure, precision, or repeatability.

&nbsp;

They are particularly useful when:

&nbsp;

• A task involves multiple reasoning stages such as understanding, analyzing, deciding, and communicating.

&nbsp;

• You need to produce tailored outputs for different audiences (executives, technical staff, public communications).

&nbsp;

• Inputs are messy or inconsistent — such as transcripts, unstructured notes, combined documents, or mixed data formats.

&nbsp;

• The task must withstand governance review, audit demands, or transparency requirements.

&nbsp;

• Processes need to scale across teams or programs without relying on one person’s intuition.

&nbsp;

A strong indicator that your process can benefit from an agentic workflow is when your team repeatedly recreates the same content, adapts it for different stakeholders, or spends considerable time cleaning up inputs. Workflows help formalize and expedite existing process as well as the informal or fragmented tasks and turn them into structured, dependable steps.

To design an agentic workflow, it is helpful to break down thinking into a set of clear cognitive roles.
The reasoning blocks provide a simple, intuitive way to do this. They are not rigid rules — instead, they serve as a mental model that mirrors how humans naturally process information.

&nbsp;

Here are the six reasoning blocks:

&nbsp;

**1) Perceiver**
&nbsp;

• Takes in raw, unfiltered information.
&nbsp;

• Extracts meaning, identifies key points, and begins structuring what exists.
&nbsp;

• Comparable to noticing and understanding sensory inputs.

&nbsp;

**2) Analyzer**
&nbsp;

• Interprets and hydrates the information captured by the Perceiver.
&nbsp;

• Identifies patterns, themes, risks, gaps, or contradictions.
&nbsp;

• Provides the “thinking” stage before decisions are made.


&nbsp;

**3) Decider**
&nbsp;

• Evaluates available options or directions.
&nbsp;

• Chooses the most appropriate path forward based on evidence or constraints.
&nbsp;

• Represents decision-making and prioritization.

&nbsp;

**4) Generator**
&nbsp;

• Produces structured content derived from the chosen direction.
&nbsp;

• Creates plans, summaries, analysis documents, or other deliverables.
&nbsp;

• Converts decisions into tangible outputs.

&nbsp;

**5) Communicator**
&nbsp;

• Adapts or reframes content for specific audiences.
&nbsp;

• Adjusts tone, structure, and emphasis based on who will consume the information.
&nbsp;

• Ensures outputs are accessible and aligned to their audience.

&nbsp;

**6) Evaluator**
&nbsp;

• Reviews outputs for accuracy, quality, completeness, and alignment.
&nbsp;

• Validates whether the output meets the intended standards.
&nbsp;

• Functions as quality assurance and final refinement.

&nbsp;

Understanding these blocks is important because they can map directly to how workflows are designed and executed.

&nbsp;

**Note:** Human evaluation of the outputs at each stage is important to ensure accuracy and alignment with expectations.

Designing a workflow involves mapping the six reasoning blocks to the steps your process needs.

&nbsp;

To build a workflow:

&nbsp;

• Identify which blocks your task requires (e.g., an analysis stage, a decision stage, a communication stage).

&nbsp;

• Determine the order in which these steps should occur.

&nbsp;

• Recognize where blocks need to repeat (e.g., multiple rounds of analysis or communication).

&nbsp;

• Consider whether the workflow needs to branch for different outputs or audiences.

&nbsp;

• Ensure the sequence logically moves from raw input → structured insight → final deliverables.

&nbsp;

When linked together, these blocks form an end-to-end reasoning process that transforms unstructured or fragmented information into clear, repeatable, high-quality results. At this stage, you are no longer just writing prompts — you are intentionally designing an AI-assisted reasoning system.

Specialization of agents and repeatability of an entire process is the core advantage of agentic workflows.

&nbsp;

By limiting each block to one specific cognitive role, the workflow increases precision and reduces ambiguity. When AI is not burdened with conflicting instructions, its reasoning becomes clearer and its outputs become more reliable.

&nbsp;

Each reasoning block can be further enhanced using prompting frameworks such as:

&nbsp;

• **Super Prompts:** Deep, highly detailed instructions.

&nbsp;

• **RICECO:** A structure that clarifies role, instruction, context, examples, constraints, and outputs.

&nbsp;

• **TRUST:** A consistency and quality-focused framework that helps reduce drift.

&nbsp;

Because the blocks are modular and well-defined, workflows can scale from simple tasks to highly complex, multi-stage systems without losing clarity. This allows an organization to build processes that are consistent across teams and use cases.

This example demonstrates how the reasoning blocks work together to handle a messy input: a meeting transcript.

&nbsp;

The desired outputs — an executive summary, a technical summary, a structured project plan, and a final quality review — require several distinct types of reasoning.

&nbsp;

Trying to generate all of these outputs in a single step overwhelms the AI. The model would be forced to perceive, analyze, decide, generate, and tailor content simultaneously, which leads to inconsistency and lower quality.

&nbsp;

Instead, the example breaks the task into nine steps. Each step corresponds to a specific reasoning block.

&nbsp;

By doing one thing at a time, the workflow ensures that the final deliverables are clearer, more accurate, and consistently aligned to both executive and technical needs.

This example provides a detailed look at the complete workflow and how it aligns to each reasoning block.

&nbsp;

• The **Perceiver** reads the transcript and extracts the core meaning.

&nbsp;

• The first **Analyzer** identifies and hydrates requirements, gaps, risks, and missing information.

&nbsp;

• The second **Analyzer** conducts wide research, incorporating external sources, best practices, standards, and potential providers or solutions.

&nbsp;

• The **Decider** selects the recommended direction based on the evidence gathered.

&nbsp;

• The **Generator** converts the chosen direction into a structured project plan.

&nbsp;

• The first **Communicator** creates the executive summary tailored for leadership.

&nbsp;

• The second **Communicator** produces the technical version tailored for implementers.

&nbsp;

• The **Evaluator** reviews the project plan for completeness, alignment, and overall quality.

&nbsp;

This demonstrates a complete reasoning pathway:

**perceive → analyze → decide → generate → communicate → evaluate.**

&nbsp;

The workflow turns human-like reasoning into a structured system that AI can replicate reliably.

Welcome to **Mastering Prompting,** this module is designed to focus on the practical application of prompt engineering. We'll be breaking down two key types of prompts: **"micro prompts"** and **"super prompts"**. 

&nbsp; 

This session will provide you with the core concepts needed to help you improve your daily work by guiding your AI assistant responsibly to provide more effective high quality responses,

**What is a Micro Prompt?**

&nbsp; 

A micro prompt is a short, direct, and task-specific instruction given to an AI model to get a quick, concise response.

&nbsp; 

Think of it as a simple command. These prompts are ideal for efficiency and are best suited for tasks that do not require deep analysis or extensive context. They are the workhorses of everyday AI interaction, helping to automate small tasks and retrieve information swiftly

**What is a Super Prompt?**

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

It often involves assigning a role to the AI, providing detailed background information, specifying the desired format, and outlining multiple sub-tasks. Super prompts are used when the quality, depth, and structure of the output are more important than the speed of the response.

**Key Differences at a Glance**

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

The choice between a micro and a super prompt depends entirely on the task at hand. The table below provides a clear comparison to help guide that decision,

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

 *(please expand the image to view the entire table)*

**When to Use Which: A Decision Framework**

&nbsp;  

To help you here is a simple decision-making framework. The choice depends on three key factors:

&nbsp;  

 **Task Complexity, Time Availability, and Desired Output Quality**.

**Use a Micro Prompt when:**

• The task is simple and straightforward (e.g., defining a term, summarizing a short text).
&nbsp;  
• You need an answer quickly and are willing to sacrifice depth for speed.
&nbsp;  
• The output does not need to be in a specific format.

&nbsp;  


**Use a Super Prompt when:**

&nbsp;  

• The task is complex and requires nuanced understanding or analysis.
&nbsp;  
• You have time to invest in crafting a detailed prompt to ensure a high-quality result.
&nbsp;  
• The output must be structured, formatted, or adhere to specific constraints (e.g., a report, a policy brief, a detailed email).

&nbsp;

Now that you’ve seen **Micro Prompts** and **Super Prompts** in action, you’re ready to start applying them to your own work. 

&nbsp; 

Remember: choose **Micro Prompts for quick, focused answers**, and **Super Prompts when you need depth, structure, and precision**. With consistent use, these techniques will help you work faster, produce higher-quality outputs, and get far more value from your AI tools.

This section is a designed as a practical, step-by-step walkthrough where we'll move from theory to practice. We highly recommend opening your preferred AI assistant (like Albert, M365 Copilot, or Gemini) to try these prompts yourself. 

&nbsp;

Following along is the best way to see the difference in real-time. We'll start by testing a few simple **micro prompts** to see how they handle fast, simple tasks. Then, we'll demonstrate the real power of a **super prompt** by building one from scratch.

**Part 1: Using Micro Prompts**

&nbsp;

 Let's start with two simple **micro prompts**. Remember, these are your tools for **efficiency and speed**. You use them when you need a quick, factual answer or want to generate a simple list of ideas without needing a lot of detail or specific formatting. Let's try two common public sector examples: getting a definition and brainstorming challenges.

**Demo 1: Getting a Definition**

&nbsp;

**Open your AI chat tool.**

&nbsp;

Type or copy and paste the following prompt, then press Enter:

&nbsp;

*Define 'stakeholder engagement' in the context of public policy.*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** You should get a fast, factual, and clear definition. It's not formatted in any special way, but it gets the job done quickly. This is a perfect use for a micro prompt.

**Demo 2: Quick Brainstorming**

&nbsp;

**Open your AI chat tool.**

&nbsp;

Type or copy and paste the following prompt, then press Enter:

&nbsp;

*List ten potential challenges for implementing a new city-wide recycling program..*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** In just a few seconds, the AI should generate a solid list of ideas. This is a great starting point for a meeting or a report. The goal was speed and efficiency, not a deep analysis, so a micro prompt was the right choice.

**Part 2: The "Micro to Super" Upgrade**

&nbsp;

Now we'll show you how to upgrade a weak micro prompt into a powerful **super prompt**. This "before and after" example will show you why adding **context, roles, and structure** is so important . We'll start by running a common but vague micro prompt to see the generic, unhelpful response it produces. Then, we will build a high-quality super prompt step-by-step to tackle the exact same task and compare the results.

**Step 1: The "Before" (The Weak Micro Prompt)**

&nbsp;

Type the following vague, one-line prompt into your AI tool:

&nbsp;

*Write an email about the new flexible work policy.*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** Look closely at what the AI gives you. It will likely be very generic. It might say "Dear Team," use placeholders like [Insert details here], and have a generic closing. This isn't very helpful and creates more editing work for you.

**Step 2: The "After" (Building the Super Prompt)**

&nbsp;

Now, let's build a super prompt to get a result that is 90% complete, not 10%. We will add specific components one by one.

&nbsp;

**Tip:** When building your own super prompts, think of these components as a checklist: Role, Context, Structure, Details, and Tone.

&nbsp;

**Assign a Role:** Tell the AI who it should be. 

&nbsp;

*Act as an HR Manager.*

&nbsp;

**Provide Context:** Explain the situation and the goal. 

&nbsp;

*Our department is launching a new flexible work policy. The new policy allows employees to work from home two days a week. The goal of this email is to inform all staff clearly and positively.*

&nbsp;

**Define the Structure:** Tell the AI exactly how to format the output.

&nbsp;

*Structure the email with:*

&nbsp;

• *A clear, positive subject line.*



• *An introduction explaining the purpose of the email.*



• *A section explaining the main policy details.*



• *A closing that invites questions.*`

&nbsp;

**Add Specific Details:** Give the AI the key facts to prevent placeholders.

&nbsp;

*the official start date is the first of next month. Direct employees to the full policy document on the intranet and to contact their direct manager for questions.*

&nbsp;

**Set the Tone:** Specify the feeling you want the email to convey. 

&nbsp;

*The tone should be professional yet supportive.*

**Step 3: Run the Full Super Prompt**

&nbsp;

Now, let's combine all those pieces into one complete super prompt. Copy and paste the entire text below into your AI tool and press Enter.  
&nbsp;  

*Act as an HR Manager. Our department is launching a new flexible work policy. The new policy allows employees to work from home two days a week. The goal of this email is to inform all staff clearly and positively.*

&nbsp;  

*Structure the email with:*

&nbsp;  



• *A clear, positive subject line.*



• *An introduction explaining the purpose of the email.*



• *A section explaining the main policy details.*



• *A closing that invites questions.*`

&nbsp;  

*Mention the official start date is the first of next month. Direct employees to the full policy document on the intranet and to contact their direct manager for questions. The tone should be professional yet supportive.*

**Step 4: Compare the Results**

&nbsp;

Look at this new output and compare it to the first "before" email.

&nbsp;

You should see a massive improvement. This version will have a clear subject line, a positive introduction, the specific policy details we provided, and the correct contacts. The tone will be professional and supportive, just as we asked.

&nbsp;

This is an email you can copy, paste, and send with minimal edits. By investing 30 more seconds to write a detailed prompt, you saved yourself 10-15 minutes of writing and editing.

&nbsp;

**That is the power of a super prompt.**

&nbsp;

After running both prompts, you can clearly see the difference. The first generic email created more work, while the super prompt delivered a 90% complete draft that you could use immediately. That is the power of investing an extra 30 seconds in your prompt: you get precise, high-quality, and structured results that meet your specific needs.

