Welcome to **Mastering Prompting,** this module is designed to focus on the practical application of prompt engineering. We'll be breaking down two key types of prompts: **"micro prompts"** and **"super prompts"**. 

&nbsp; 

This session will provide you with the core concepts needed to help you improve your daily work by guiding your AI assistant responsibly to provide more effective high quality responses,

**What is a Micro Prompt?**

&nbsp; 

A micro prompt is a short, direct, and task-specific instruction given to an AI model to get a quick, concise response.

&nbsp; 

Think of it as a simple command. These prompts are ideal for efficiency and are best suited for tasks that do not require deep analysis or extensive context. They are the workhorses of everyday AI interaction, helping to automate small tasks and retrieve information swiftly

**What is a Super Prompt?**

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

It often involves assigning a role to the AI, providing detailed background information, specifying the desired format, and outlining multiple sub-tasks. Super prompts are used when the quality, depth, and structure of the output are more important than the speed of the response.

**Key Differences at a Glance**

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

The choice between a micro and a super prompt depends entirely on the task at hand. The table below provides a clear comparison to help guide that decision,

&nbsp; 

A super prompt is a detailed, multi-step, and context-rich instruction designed to guide an AI model toward producing a complex, structured, and high-quality output.

&nbsp; 

 *(please expand the image to view the entire table)*

**When to Use Which: A Decision Framework**

&nbsp;  

To help you here is a simple decision-making framework. The choice depends on three key factors:

&nbsp;  

 **Task Complexity, Time Availability, and Desired Output Quality**.

**Use a Micro Prompt when:**

• The task is simple and straightforward (e.g., defining a term, summarizing a short text).
&nbsp;  
• You need an answer quickly and are willing to sacrifice depth for speed.
&nbsp;  
• The output does not need to be in a specific format.

&nbsp;  


**Use a Super Prompt when:**

&nbsp;  

• The task is complex and requires nuanced understanding or analysis.
&nbsp;  
• You have time to invest in crafting a detailed prompt to ensure a high-quality result.
&nbsp;  
• The output must be structured, formatted, or adhere to specific constraints (e.g., a report, a policy brief, a detailed email).

&nbsp;

Now that you’ve seen **Micro Prompts** and **Super Prompts** in action, you’re ready to start applying them to your own work. 

&nbsp; 

Remember: choose **Micro Prompts for quick, focused answers**, and **Super Prompts when you need depth, structure, and precision**. With consistent use, these techniques will help you work faster, produce higher-quality outputs, and get far more value from your AI tools.

This section is a designed as a practical, step-by-step walkthrough where we'll move from theory to practice. We highly recommend opening your preferred AI assistant (like Albert, M365 Copilot, or Gemini) to try these prompts yourself. 

&nbsp;

Following along is the best way to see the difference in real-time. We'll start by testing a few simple **micro prompts** to see how they handle fast, simple tasks. Then, we'll demonstrate the real power of a **super prompt** by building one from scratch.

**Part 1: Using Micro Prompts**

&nbsp;

 Let's start with two simple **micro prompts**. Remember, these are your tools for **efficiency and speed**. You use them when you need a quick, factual answer or want to generate a simple list of ideas without needing a lot of detail or specific formatting. Let's try two common public sector examples: getting a definition and brainstorming challenges.

**Demo 1: Getting a Definition**

&nbsp;

**Open your AI chat tool.**

&nbsp;

Type or copy and paste the following prompt, then press Enter:

&nbsp;

*Define 'stakeholder engagement' in the context of public policy.*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** You should get a fast, factual, and clear definition. It's not formatted in any special way, but it gets the job done quickly. This is a perfect use for a micro prompt.

**Demo 2: Quick Brainstorming**

&nbsp;

**Open your AI chat tool.**

&nbsp;

Type or copy and paste the following prompt, then press Enter:

&nbsp;

*List ten potential challenges for implementing a new city-wide recycling program..*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** In just a few seconds, the AI should generate a solid list of ideas. This is a great starting point for a meeting or a report. The goal was speed and efficiency, not a deep analysis, so a micro prompt was the right choice.

**Part 2: The "Micro to Super" Upgrade**

&nbsp;

Now we'll show you how to upgrade a weak micro prompt into a powerful **super prompt**. This "before and after" example will show you why adding **context, roles, and structure** is so important . We'll start by running a common but vague micro prompt to see the generic, unhelpful response it produces. Then, we will build a high-quality super prompt step-by-step to tackle the exact same task and compare the results.

**Step 1: The "Before" (The Weak Micro Prompt)**

&nbsp;

Type the following vague, one-line prompt into your AI tool:

&nbsp;

*Write an email about the new flexible work policy.*

&nbsp;

**Review the output:** Look closely at what the AI gives you. It will likely be very generic. It might say "Dear Team," use placeholders like [Insert details here], and have a generic closing. This isn't very helpful and creates more editing work for you.

**Step 2: The "After" (Building the Super Prompt)**

&nbsp;

Now, let's build a super prompt to get a result that is 90% complete, not 10%. We will add specific components one by one.

&nbsp;

**Tip:** When building your own super prompts, think of these components as a checklist: Role, Context, Structure, Details, and Tone.

&nbsp;

**Assign a Role:** Tell the AI who it should be. 

&nbsp;

*Act as an HR Manager.*

&nbsp;

**Provide Context:** Explain the situation and the goal. 

&nbsp;

*Our department is launching a new flexible work policy. The new policy allows employees to work from home two days a week. The goal of this email is to inform all staff clearly and positively.*

&nbsp;

**Define the Structure:** Tell the AI exactly how to format the output.

&nbsp;

*Structure the email with:*

&nbsp;

• *A clear, positive subject line.*



• *An introduction explaining the purpose of the email.*



• *A section explaining the main policy details.*



• *A closing that invites questions.*`

&nbsp;

**Add Specific Details:** Give the AI the key facts to prevent placeholders.

&nbsp;

*the official start date is the first of next month. Direct employees to the full policy document on the intranet and to contact their direct manager for questions.*

&nbsp;

**Set the Tone:** Specify the feeling you want the email to convey. 

&nbsp;

*The tone should be professional yet supportive.*

**Step 3: Run the Full Super Prompt**

&nbsp;

Now, let's combine all those pieces into one complete super prompt. Copy and paste the entire text below into your AI tool and press Enter.  
&nbsp;  

*Act as an HR Manager. Our department is launching a new flexible work policy. The new policy allows employees to work from home two days a week. The goal of this email is to inform all staff clearly and positively.*

&nbsp;  

*Structure the email with:*

&nbsp;  



• *A clear, positive subject line.*



• *An introduction explaining the purpose of the email.*



• *A section explaining the main policy details.*



• *A closing that invites questions.*`

&nbsp;  

*Mention the official start date is the first of next month. Direct employees to the full policy document on the intranet and to contact their direct manager for questions. The tone should be professional yet supportive.*

**Step 4: Compare the Results**

&nbsp;

Look at this new output and compare it to the first "before" email.

&nbsp;

You should see a massive improvement. This version will have a clear subject line, a positive introduction, the specific policy details we provided, and the correct contacts. The tone will be professional and supportive, just as we asked.

&nbsp;

This is an email you can copy, paste, and send with minimal edits. By investing 30 more seconds to write a detailed prompt, you saved yourself 10-15 minutes of writing and editing.

&nbsp;

**That is the power of a super prompt.**

&nbsp;

After running both prompts, you can clearly see the difference. The first generic email created more work, while the super prompt delivered a 90% complete draft that you could use immediately. That is the power of investing an extra 30 seconds in your prompt: you get precise, high-quality, and structured results that meet your specific needs.

