**The Most Important Skill: Moving from "Clicks" to "Concepts"**

&nbsp;  

Before we begin building our new AI Specialist, lets look at the 4 pillars to creating a Gem.

&nbsp;  

The technical part of creating a Gem is incredibly simple. It involves just a few clicks: "New Gem," typing in a name, and clicking "Save". The real skill, and the focus of this training, is writing the **Instructions**.   

&nbsp;  

The quality of your Gem's output is 100% dependent on the quality of your instructions. This process is sometimes called "prompt engineering" , but a simpler way to think about it is "writing a good job description." 

&nbsp;  

To write great instructions, you do not need to be a technical expert. You just need to be clear about what you want. Google's own guidelines recommend a simple, four-part framework, which we will call the "Four Pillars". Mastering these four pillars is the key to building effective Gems.

**Pillar 1: PERSONA (Giving Your Gem a Job Title)**

&nbsp;  

• **Concept:** This answers the question, "Who are you?"

&nbsp;  

Telling the AI to "act as if"  it is a specific expert is the fastest way to get focused, high-quality responses. A persona sets the tone, style, and knowledge base for the AI.   

&nbsp; 

•  **Weak Instruction:** "Help me with writing."
&nbsp;

•  **Strong Instruction:** "You are an expert editor specializing in public sector communications and plain language."
&nbsp;

•	**Weak Instruction:** "Summarize this."
&nbsp;  

•	**Strong Instruction:** "You are a professional executive assistant responsible for creating concise, accurate, and formal meeting minutes".   

&nbsp;  

**Pillar 2: TASK (Giving Your Gem a Clear Action)**

&nbsp;  

• **Concept:** This answers the question, "What must you do?"

&nbsp;  

This is the primary action you want the Gem to perform. Be as specific and direct as possible. Use clear, strong verbs. It is also a best practice to avoid negative instructions (e.g., "Don't use jargon"). Instead, state what you do want (e.g., "You must use simple, everyday language").     

&nbsp; 

•  **Weak Instruction:** "Review this text."
&nbsp;

•  **Strong Instruction:** "You must **rewrite** the provided text into simple, plain language at a 6th-grade reading level".
&nbsp;

•	**Weak Instruction:** "Tell me about this document."
&nbsp;

•	**Strong Instruction:** "You must **summarize** the key points, **identify** all decisions made, and **extract** all action items from this transcript". 

&nbsp;  

**Pillar 3: CONTEXT (Giving Your Gem the "Unwritten Rules")**

&nbsp;  

• **Concept:** This answers the question, "What secret knowledge do you need to do this job properly?"

&nbsp;  

This is arguably the most critical pillar for public sector work. This is where you provide the "unwritten rules" of your job the specific audience, the required tone, and the constraints that a generic AI would not know.   

&nbsp; 

• **Audience Example:** "The audience for this rewrite is the general public, who have no prior knowledge of this policy".
&nbsp;

• **Tone Example:** "The tone must be professional, polite, and highly empathetic, even if the citizen is angry".
&nbsp;

• **Constraint Example:** "You must avoid all bureaucratic jargon, undefined acronyms, and passive-aggressive phrases". "You must strictly adhere to the provided style guide." 

&nbsp;  

**Pillar 4: FORMAT (Telling Your Gem What the Final Product Should Look Like)**

&nbsp;  

• **Concept:** This answers the question, "What is my deliverable?"

&nbsp;  

Never assume the AI will give you the output in the format you need. You must specify the desired structure. This is where you save yourself hours of reformatting.  

&nbsp; 

• **Example 1:** "Provide the output as a two-column table, with 'Original' in the first column and 'Simplified' in the second." 
&nbsp;

• **Example 2:** "The summary must be a bulleted list of no more than 5 key points".
&nbsp;

• **Example 3:** "Format the response as a formal briefing note with the following exact headings: 'Issue,' 'Background,' 'Analysis,' and 'Recommendation'". 

&nbsp;

Think of a **"Gem"** as a Department Specialist that is already trained for their role.

&nbsp;  

Instead of explaining the same rules every time you start a chat (e.g., "Please summarize this using plain language," "Use our department's format," "Don't use jargon"), you "teach" the Gem these rules once. Then, whenever you need that specific task done, you just click on that Gem, and it already knows exactly what to do.

**Part 1: Step-by-Step Walkthrough**
**Scenario:** We will build a Gem called "The Plain Language Pro."

&nbsp; 

**Goal:** This Gem will take complex government policy documents or emails and rewrite them so they are easy for the general public to understand (Grade 8 reading level), which is a common requirement in the public sector.

&nbsp;

**Phase 1: Open the Gem Manager**

&nbsp;  

