**1. What Automated Business Case Creation Actually Is**
&nbsp;  


Automated Business Case Creation is a **dual-track agentic process** used to rapidly synthesize two distinct streams of information::

&nbsp;  

• **External Reality:** Market research, vendor landscapes, and technological standards.
&nbsp;  

• **Internal Reality:** Ministry mandates, stakeholder transcripts, and project objectives.  
&nbsp;  

This is the **integration layer** where public data meets internal strategy. The system doesn't just write; it verifies gaps between what you want to do and what is *feasible or required.*

&nbsp;

The goal is simple: **Transform raw interviews and mandates into a standardized, rigorous business case draft that aligns with government priorities and is ready for redlining**.

&nbsp;

**2. Why We Do It in Government**
&nbsp;

Developing a business case is often time-consuming because it requires mapping complex ideas to specific government priorities. This workflow supports:

&nbsp;

• **Strategic Alignment:** Anchoring projects directly to ministry mandate letters to justify why the work is necessary.
&nbsp;

• **Gap Analysis:** Identifying missing critical criteria (e.g., budget, timelines) early by comparing transcripts against required fields.
&nbsp;

• **Evidence-Based Planning:** Grounding internal goals in the reality of today’s market through extensive web research.
&nbsp;

• **Standardization:** Ensuring every business case follows a clear, repeatable structure, regardless of the author.

&nbsp;

**3. Why Traditional Research Slows Teams Down**

&nbsp;

• **Manual Interviews & Notes:** Relying on memory or disorganized meeting notes.
&nbsp;

• **Disconnected Research:** Googling solutions separately from drafting the narrative.
&nbsp;

• **Inconsistent Formatting:** "Frankenstein" documents that vary wildly depending on who wrote them.
&nbsp;

• Missed Mandates: Failing to explicitly link project goals to Cabinet or Ministry priorities.

&nbsp;

This leads to:

&nbsp;

• **Long development cycles.**
&nbsp;

• **Gaps in logic or budget planning.**
&nbsp;

• **Drafts that lack "meat" or evidence.**

&nbsp;

**Agentic workflows fix that by splitting the work into two streams—research and extraction—and merging them into a consistent, high-quality draft.**

&nbsp;

**4. The Standard Output: A Corporate/Biographical Dossier**

A complete business case generated by this workflow captures the essentials:

&nbsp;

• **Executive Summary:** A high-level overview of the initiative.
&nbsp;

• **Strategic Alignment:** Explicit links to Ministry mandates and Cabinet direction.
&nbsp;

• **Objectives:** Clear goals like "enhance data accuracy" or "accelerate compliance".
&nbsp;

• **Recommended Options:** Analysis of build vs. buy, including vendor selection strategies.
&nbsp;

• **Solution Architecture:** Core technologies, security standards (ISO/SOC2), and mobile-first designs.
&nbsp;

• **Risks & Issues:** Identification of potential pitfalls (e.g., privacy compliance).
&nbsp;

• **Timeline & Budget:** Financial projections and implementation schedules.

&nbsp;

This structure ensures that every draft provides a solid foundation for the product team and procurement to build upon.

&nbsp;

**5. How Agentic AI Changes the Game**
&nbsp;

Instead of a single writer trying to do everything, this workflow employs a **Dual-Track Implementation:**

&nbsp;

**1.	Stream A (The Researcher):** Casts a wide net on the internet, reading ~200 websites to build a bibliography and evidence base.
&nbsp;

**2. Stream B (The Extractor):** "Listens" to your transcripts and reads your mandate letters to extract specific tags and identify gaps.
&nbsp;

**3. The Convergence:** A senior "Business Analyst" agent merges these two streams, using the research to flesh out the internal goals into a professional document.

&nbsp;

This turns a process that takes weeks into a short execution time, producing a draft that is grounded in reality and aligned with strategy.

&nbsp;

**6. Why This Matters for Public-Sector Excellence**
&nbsp;

Good business cases are the foundation of good projects. By using this agentic workflow, we ensure:

&nbsp;

**• Consistency:** Every project uses the same rigorous template.
&nbsp;

**• Speed:** "Moving from "idea" to "draft" in minutes, not weeks.
&nbsp;

**• Alignment:** Mandate letters are never an afterthought; they are baked into the drafting process.
&nbsp;

**• Agility:** We can rapidly "redline" and regenerate the case as new information comes to light.

&nbsp;

**Business Case Creator Workflow**
&nbsp;

This workflow follows a "fan-out/fan-in" pattern with two distinct input sources (Web & Internal Data) that converge at the final drafting stage.

&nbsp;

**Stream A: The Research Factory**

&nbsp;

**Stage 1 — Search Criteria Creator**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** Strategic Scoping Agent

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Develops 20 distinct search criteria based on the initial prompt.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**

&nbsp;

**-** Reads the high-level business problem.
&nbsp;

**-** Generates diverse terms (e.g., "offender supervision," "mobile transcription").
&nbsp;

**-** Allows for human editing/locking of terms.

&nbsp;

• **Why this agent exists:** It expands the concept beyond a single keyword to ensure comprehensive market coverage.

&nbsp;

**Stage 2 — Web Research Swarm (Brave Search & Scrapers)**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** Data Acquisition Layer

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Executes searches and downloads content from ~200 websites.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**
&nbsp;

**- Brave Search:** Finds 10 results per search term.
&nbsp;

**- Web Scrapers:** Download and clean the content from those results.
&nbsp;

**- Web Researchers:** 10 agents synthesize the raw web data into a coherent research report.

&nbsp;

• **Why they exist:** They provide the "meat" and external evidence to support the business case.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

**Stream B: The Internal Analyst**

&nbsp;


**Stage 3 — Content Ingestion (Transcripts & Mandates)**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** Data Input

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Loads the raw "voice of the client" and strategic direction.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**
&nbsp;

**-** Ingests the Ministry Mandate Letter (strategic anchor).
&nbsp;

**-** Ingests Client Transcripts (operational needs).

&nbsp;


• **Why this agent exists:** It ensures the business case is not generic but specific to the organization's actual needs.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;


**Stage 4 — Business Information Extractor**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** Structured Analyst

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Maps unstructured conversation to specific business case fields.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**
&nbsp;

**-** Scans materials for specific tags (e.g., "Time Constraint," "Stakeholders," "Budget").
&nbsp;

**-** Fills in a structured table.
&nbsp;

**- Crucially:** Identifies gaps (e.g., "We didn't talk about options analysis") so you can ask the client before drafting.

&nbsp;


• **Why this agent exists:** It acts as a quality gate, ensuring all necessary criteria are present before drafting begins.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;

**Stream C: The Convergence**

&nbsp;


**Stage 5 — Business Analysis Super Prompt**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** The Lead Author

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Synthesizes Stream A (Research) and Stream B (Extraction) into the final document.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**
&nbsp;

**-** Takes the "tags" extracted from the transcript
&nbsp;

**-** Injects the external market research.
&nbsp;

**-** Follows a strict Markdown/JSON template to write the prose.
&nbsp;

**-** Updates outdated domain knowledge (e.g., replacing FOIP with ATIA).

&nbsp;

• **Why this agent exists:** It ensures every business case follows the same high-quality pattern and tone.

&nbsp;

&nbsp;


**Stage 6 — Word Export Agent**

&nbsp;

• **Role:** Publishing Layer

&nbsp;

• **Function:** Converts the Markdown draft into a standard Word document.

&nbsp;

• **Behavior:**
&nbsp;

**-** Generates the final .docx file for download.

&nbsp;

• **Why this agent exists:** To provide a tangible artifact that can be redlined and shared with partners.

