Agentic workflows are valuable because they introduce a level of structure and discipline that generic AI prompting lacks.

&nbsp;

**Consistency:**
When work is divided into well-defined steps, the output becomes more stable. Each agent focuses on one role, so the risk of drift, inconsistency, or hallucination drops significantly.

&nbsp;


**Quality:**
Assigning a single responsibility to each stage improves clarity and accuracy. The workflow reduces cognitive overload — both for the AI and for humans — by ensuring that each stage handles only one mental function.

&nbsp;

**Structure & Traceability:**
Workflows create a transparent reasoning path. This allows teams to understand exactly how an output was produced, making it easier to verify, audit, or refine the process. This is especially important in decision support, policy development, governance, and public-sector accountability.

&nbsp;

**Specialization:**
Complex tasks become manageable when they are decomposed into smaller, focused components. Each component performs better because it concentrates on one cognitive role rather than trying to perform many simultaneously.

&nbsp;

Together, these benefits help organizations build scalable, reliable AI-supported processes that improve both productivity and work quality.

Agentic workflows are most effective in situations where work naturally unfolds in multiple stages or where the task requires structure, precision, or repeatability.

&nbsp;

They are particularly useful when:

&nbsp;

• A task involves multiple reasoning stages such as understanding, analyzing, deciding, and communicating.

&nbsp;

• You need to produce tailored outputs for different audiences (executives, technical staff, public communications).

&nbsp;

• Inputs are messy or inconsistent — such as transcripts, unstructured notes, combined documents, or mixed data formats.

&nbsp;

• The task must withstand governance review, audit demands, or transparency requirements.

&nbsp;

• Processes need to scale across teams or programs without relying on one person’s intuition.

&nbsp;

A strong indicator that your process can benefit from an agentic workflow is when your team repeatedly recreates the same content, adapts it for different stakeholders, or spends considerable time cleaning up inputs. Workflows help formalize and expedite existing process as well as the informal or fragmented tasks and turn them into structured, dependable steps.

To design an agentic workflow, it is helpful to break down thinking into a set of clear cognitive roles.
The reasoning blocks provide a simple, intuitive way to do this. They are not rigid rules — instead, they serve as a mental model that mirrors how humans naturally process information.

&nbsp;

Here are the six reasoning blocks:

&nbsp;

**1) Perceiver**
&nbsp;

• Takes in raw, unfiltered information.
&nbsp;

• Extracts meaning, identifies key points, and begins structuring what exists.
&nbsp;

• Comparable to noticing and understanding sensory inputs.

&nbsp;

**2) Analyzer**
&nbsp;

• Interprets and hydrates the information captured by the Perceiver.
&nbsp;

• Identifies patterns, themes, risks, gaps, or contradictions.
&nbsp;

• Provides the “thinking” stage before decisions are made.


&nbsp;

**3) Decider**
&nbsp;

• Evaluates available options or directions.
&nbsp;

• Chooses the most appropriate path forward based on evidence or constraints.
&nbsp;

• Represents decision-making and prioritization.

&nbsp;

**4) Generator**
&nbsp;

• Produces structured content derived from the chosen direction.
&nbsp;

• Creates plans, summaries, analysis documents, or other deliverables.
&nbsp;

• Converts decisions into tangible outputs.

&nbsp;

**5) Communicator**
&nbsp;

• Adapts or reframes content for specific audiences.
&nbsp;

• Adjusts tone, structure, and emphasis based on who will consume the information.
&nbsp;

• Ensures outputs are accessible and aligned to their audience.

&nbsp;

**6) Evaluator**
&nbsp;

• Reviews outputs for accuracy, quality, completeness, and alignment.
&nbsp;

• Validates whether the output meets the intended standards.
&nbsp;

• Functions as quality assurance and final refinement.

&nbsp;

Understanding these blocks is important because they can map directly to how workflows are designed and executed.

&nbsp;

**Note:** Human evaluation of the outputs at each stage is important to ensure accuracy and alignment with expectations.

Designing a workflow involves mapping the six reasoning blocks to the steps your process needs.

&nbsp;

To build a workflow:

&nbsp;

• Identify which blocks your task requires (e.g., an analysis stage, a decision stage, a communication stage).

&nbsp;

• Determine the order in which these steps should occur.

&nbsp;

• Recognize where blocks need to repeat (e.g., multiple rounds of analysis or communication).

&nbsp;

• Consider whether the workflow needs to branch for different outputs or audiences.

&nbsp;

• Ensure the sequence logically moves from raw input → structured insight → final deliverables.

&nbsp;

When linked together, these blocks form an end-to-end reasoning process that transforms unstructured or fragmented information into clear, repeatable, high-quality results. At this stage, you are no longer just writing prompts — you are intentionally designing an AI-assisted reasoning system.

Specialization of agents and repeatability of an entire process is the core advantage of agentic workflows.

&nbsp;

By limiting each block to one specific cognitive role, the workflow increases precision and reduces ambiguity. When AI is not burdened with conflicting instructions, its reasoning becomes clearer and its outputs become more reliable.

&nbsp;

Each reasoning block can be further enhanced using prompting frameworks such as:

&nbsp;

• **Super Prompts:** Deep, highly detailed instructions.

&nbsp;

• **RICECO:** A structure that clarifies role, instruction, context, examples, constraints, and outputs.

&nbsp;

• **TRUST:** A consistency and quality-focused framework that helps reduce drift.

&nbsp;

Because the blocks are modular and well-defined, workflows can scale from simple tasks to highly complex, multi-stage systems without losing clarity. This allows an organization to build processes that are consistent across teams and use cases.

This example demonstrates how the reasoning blocks work together to handle a messy input: a meeting transcript.

&nbsp;

The desired outputs — an executive summary, a technical summary, a structured project plan, and a final quality review — require several distinct types of reasoning.

&nbsp;

Trying to generate all of these outputs in a single step overwhelms the AI. The model would be forced to perceive, analyze, decide, generate, and tailor content simultaneously, which leads to inconsistency and lower quality.

&nbsp;

Instead, the example breaks the task into nine steps. Each step corresponds to a specific reasoning block.

&nbsp;

By doing one thing at a time, the workflow ensures that the final deliverables are clearer, more accurate, and consistently aligned to both executive and technical needs.

This example provides a detailed look at the complete workflow and how it aligns to each reasoning block.

&nbsp;

• The **Perceiver** reads the transcript and extracts the core meaning.

&nbsp;

• The first **Analyzer** identifies and hydrates requirements, gaps, risks, and missing information.

&nbsp;

• The second **Analyzer** conducts wide research, incorporating external sources, best practices, standards, and potential providers or solutions.

&nbsp;

• The **Decider** selects the recommended direction based on the evidence gathered.

&nbsp;

• The **Generator** converts the chosen direction into a structured project plan.

&nbsp;

• The first **Communicator** creates the executive summary tailored for leadership.

&nbsp;

• The second **Communicator** produces the technical version tailored for implementers.

&nbsp;

• The **Evaluator** reviews the project plan for completeness, alignment, and overall quality.

&nbsp;

This demonstrates a complete reasoning pathway:

**perceive → analyze → decide → generate → communicate → evaluate.**

&nbsp;

The workflow turns human-like reasoning into a structured system that AI can replicate reliably.

This module will address four key topics. &nbsp;  
1 - How Artificial Intelligence processes information and “thinks.” &nbsp;  
2 - Systems thinking - understanding goals, flows, feedback loops, and relationships between components. &nbsp;  
3 - The FOCUS framework to organize your work to effectively engage AI. &nbsp;  
4 - A worked example, applying the above principles to an existing manual workflow and reimagining it through the lens of AI. &nbsp;

Human beings possess a natural ability to place themselves in another person’s position and anticipate how that individual might think, behave, or act in a given situation.

&nbsp;  

This capacity, referred to as **Theory of Mind**, enables effective interaction and understanding among humans.

&nbsp;

If we strive to better understand the **mind of artificial intelligence**—how it processes information, interprets input, and produces responses—we can apply that understanding to enhance how we communicate and collaborate with AI systems.

&nbsp;

There is, however, a **fundamental distinction** between how humans and AI think.

&nbsp;

Artificial intelligence identifies **patterns** within its training data and the information provided by users, then applies **statistical probabilities** to generate its responses.

&nbsp;

Humans, by contrast, engage in **reasoning**—drawing upon their **senses**, **knowledge**, **emotions**, and **experiences** to interpret situations and make decisions.

&nbsp;

**Key takeaway:**&nbsp;

&emsp;➡️ *The more intentionally we structure our inputs to AI, the more reliable and effective the resulting outputs will be.*

**Systems thinking** is about seeing how parts connect — understanding the **goals**, **flows**, **feedback loops**, and **dependencies** that make up a larger system.

&nbsp;

A system is not merely a collection of parts; it is a set of **interacting components** working together toward a **shared goal or purpose**.

## **Where Does Information Break Down?**

Think of situations where several teams request the same information — each in slightly different ways.  
These are the **spaces between teams** where most problems tend to occur.

&nbsp;

At that point, the information begins to **fragment into silos** — it is no longer connected across the organization.  
When something changes, those updates fail to flow through the system.

&nbsp;

That is when **duplication**, **rework**, and **wasted effort** begin to appear — the symptoms of a disconnected system.

&nbsp;

A **worked example** will be shown later to illustrate how **systems thinking** and **agentic workflows** can help **reconnect those pieces** and **reduce friction** across the process.

Before engaging an **AI** or an **agent**, take a step back and examine **how your work is structured** — how information is **organized**, **connected**, and **understood**.

&nbsp;

The clearer and more **structured** your work is, the more effectively AI can collaborate with you.

&nbsp;

That is precisely what the **FOCUS framework** is designed to help you achieve next.

## **Example: Level 1 — M365 Copilot Project Status Agent**

This example comes from **Level 1** — the **M365 Copilot Project Status Agent** developed in **SharePoint Online**.

&nbsp;

It demonstrates how the solution aligns seamlessly with the **F.O.C.U.S. Framework** to structure the work effectively.

&nbsp;

Staff were frequently requesting **project status updates**, and the **project manager** was spending significant time gathering and formatting data from multiple sources.

&nbsp;

By **framing the challenge as a structured problem** — identifying where the data resided, what tasks were repetitive, and how AI could automate the summarization —  
the team created an **agent** capable of **retrieving and condensing reports automatically**.

&nbsp;

This not only reduced manual effort but also ensured that updates were **consistent**, **timely**, and **accessible** across the organization.

## **Worked Example: Application Assessment Process**

&nbsp;

This example illustrates an **Application Assessment process** — the sequence followed each time a new tool or application is reviewed before approval for use.

&nbsp;

There are multiple groups involved: **Cybersecurity**, **Information Management**, **Privacy**, **Architecture**, and the **Service Owner**. Each group conducts its own review, maintains its own documents, and uses its own set of questions.

&nbsp;

At the centre of this process is the **coordinator**, who spends a considerable amount of time gathering system specifications, distributing them for review, managing feedback, and attempting to keep all stakeholders aligned.

The issue is not that these reviews exist — they are all **essential**.  
The challenge is that each group often asks **different versions of the same questions**, and in **different formats**.

&nbsp;

As a result, the process becomes less about **decision-making** and more about managing **duplication**, **repetition**, and **noise**.

&nbsp;

This is precisely the kind of process where **systems thinking** can add value — by identifying the **common inputs**, the **shared intent**, and the **feedback loops**, and then **re-imagining how these elements can connect more intelligently** to create a smoother, more efficient workflow.

## **Applying the Three Perspectives**

&nbsp;

**First — Thinking Like AI**  
The primary issue is that **Cybersecurity**, **Information Management**, and **Privacy** each ask **similar questions**, but in slightly different ways.  

&nbsp;

To **think like AI**, we must establish a **clear structure** and create **shared inputs** so that the same information can be **reused** across all reviews —  rather than being retyped, reformatted, or reinterpreted each time.

&nbsp;

**Second — Systems Thinking**  
We need to step back and view the **entire process as a whole**.  
The overarching goal is to deliver a **complete, risk-assessed approval** as efficiently as possible.  

&nbsp;

When we recognize this as a **shared data ecosystem** — rather than three independent processes — it becomes easier to identify overlaps, eliminate redundancy, and preserve context across all teams.

&nbsp;

**Finally — the F.O.C.U.S. Framework**  
This framework provides a **structured approach** for engaging AI in the process.

&nbsp;

We begin by **framing the problem** — there is repetition and duplicated effort when coordinating among multiple teams.  
The AI can access a **centralized source of information** and help produce a **unified output**.  

&nbsp;

We already have **repositories for standards and templates** that the AI can draw from.  
We can also design **domain-specific agents**, such as a **Cybersecurity Specialist Agent**, to perform focused, expert-level analysis.  

&nbsp;

The final output becomes a **consolidated sign-off package**, providing a clear, complete assessment of each application or tool.

&nbsp;

Together, these three perspectives — **Thinking Like AI**, **Systems Thinking**, and **the F.O.C.U.S. Framework** —  
create the foundation for **redesigning the workflow intelligently** and enabling more cohesive, AI-supported collaboration.

## **Reimagining the Process with AI**

Now, let us take these principles and explore what happens when we **reimagine the same process with AI in mind**.

&nbsp;

Instead of humans manually passing documents and managing feedback loops,  
we introduce **AI agents** that perform **specific, well-defined roles**, each aligned with a distinct part of the process.

&nbsp;

The **Coordinator Agent** becomes the **orchestrator** — managing the overall workflow, maintaining context, and ensuring that information flows seamlessly to where it is needed.

&nbsp;

The **Cybersecurity**, **Information Management**, and **Privacy Agents** all draw from a **shared repository** of standards, templates, and past assessments.  
This ensures that each agent operates from the same **source of truth**, eliminating duplication and inconsistency.

&nbsp;

The **Architectural Agent** integrates the results — validating **system integrity** and synthesizing findings into a **single, coherent assessment**.

&nbsp;

The **Service Owner** remains the **human-in-the-loop**, providing **oversight**, **judgment**, and **alignment** with organizational goals.  
Meanwhile, input from **Cybersecurity**, **Information Management**, **Privacy**, **Architecture**, and the **vendor** helps ensure **accuracy** and **accountability**.

&nbsp;

The result is a **workflow that is faster, more consistent, and highly scalable** —  
all while preserving the **human judgment and governance** that remain essential to responsible decision-making.

## **Finding the Signal**

In the illustration above, the **signal** represents the **goal of the system** we discussed earlier —  
but it could also symbolize the **real problem** we are trying to solve, or the **key action items** hidden within a two-hour meeting transcript.

&nbsp;

As humans, we are naturally skilled at identifying what is **wrong** or **could be improved**,  
but we are not always as effective at **articulating the core issue** or defining **what we truly need**.

&nbsp;

Our capacity to hold and process information is limited.  
When data is dispersed across multiple sources, the **true signal** often becomes **buried in the noise**.

&nbsp;

This is precisely the kind of task where **AI can provide significant value**.

&nbsp;

AI operates by **searching for the pattern of your signal** within its **embedding space** —  
a network of **vectors** that captures both the **meaning** and **sequence** of words.

&nbsp;

It then selects the **sequence of words** with the **highest statistical likelihood**, based on its **training data** and the **current context**.

&nbsp;

Through this process, AI can **identify subtle relationships** within vast amounts of **unstructured information** —  
the kind of complexity that humans would struggle to hold in working memory.

&nbsp;

However, **humans remain essential** — because only we can determine **what signal we are truly looking for**  
and **decide what to do** once we find it.
